WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.523
- [Narrator] It's September, 1993.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.390
And it's finally the weekend.
- Yay!

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:07.820
- [Woman] That's right.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.403
(dreamy music)

00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.062
♪ Ah ♪

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.780
- [Narrator] Movie tickets only cost

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.950
four dollars and 14 cents.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.210
Frasier--
- I'm listening.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.110
- [Narrator] Just premiered on NBC,

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.620
and women were finally
allowed to wear pantsuits

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.550
on the Senate floor.
(bell dings)

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.316
It's Saturday morning,
and that means one thing.

00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:29.788
♪ And they're zany ♪
- Cartoons.

00:00:29.788 --> 00:00:31.760
♪ The show ♪

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.890
- [Narrator] So at eight a.m.,
"Animaniacs" was on Fox Kids,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.690
and we scheduled a
meeting with Rob Paulsen,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.770
the guy who voiced Yakko.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.060
- Oh, sure. (clears throat)

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.090
Okay, well let's see, I'm
Yakko Warner; hello, nurse.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:44.990
- [Narrator] We wanna know about

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.460
the "Nations of the World" song.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.430
- [Rob] Well, the interesting
thing is that it only took me

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:49.674
one take to record it.

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:50.861
- [Narrator] What?

00:00:50.861 --> 00:00:52.343
♪ United States, Canada, Mexico, Panama ♪

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.012
♪ Haiti, Jamaica, Peru ♪

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:55.922
♪ Republic Dominican, Cuba, Caribbean ♪

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.562
♪ Greenland, El Salvador too ♪

00:00:57.562 --> 00:00:59.500
♪ The art project ♪

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.930
- [Narrator] For most of
us, Perler beads got left

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.580
in the 1990s alongside
HitClips and Oakley Minutes.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.676
- [Man] Cool.

00:01:06.676 --> 00:01:08.310
(phone beeping)

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.274
- [Narrator] Hello, can you hear me?

00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:10.640
- Loud and clear.
- Perfect.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.350
Kyle is an artist, but
rather than paint or pen,

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.520
he uses Perler beads, even today.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.800
- [Kyle] I am all-Perler, all the time.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.773
Perler beads were invented by
a Swedish man in the 1960s.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.180
It was originally a therapy
material for older folks.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.350
- [Narrator] Kyle sees your
'90s Perler bead posters

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.790
and raises you this.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.070
- [Kyle] I would say, without a doubt,

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.060
my most ambitious project has
got to be Project Springfield,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.680
which is 40-square-foot mural
of the Simpsons' hometown.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.720
It's over 140,000
individually placed beads.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.270
And it took me about two years.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.381
- [Narrator] It's your
nostalgia, only elevated.

00:01:46.381 --> 00:01:47.611
♪ The birthday parties ♪

00:01:47.611 --> 00:01:49.700
- [Kids] Happy birthday!

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.930
- [Narrator] The holy trinity

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.030
of the '90s kids'
birthday party was simple.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.131
Pizza, cake, and laser tag.
(noisemaker toots)

00:01:56.131 --> 00:01:57.410
- [Kids] Pew pew, pew pew!

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.350
- [Narrator] Amen; so we talked

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.220
to the man responsible for all of that.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.860
- Hi, I'm George Carter,
I invented laser tag

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.280
about 30 years ago.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.470
- [Narrator] The game was
inspired by a pretty famous movie.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:09.540
- [George] The movie "Star Wars."

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.780
I had watched them going up
and down the halls of the ship

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.240
shooting lasers at one another.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.270
And I thought I'd put it
in some sort of context

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.040
and make rules, and we
got a great game then.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.830
It was more competitive
back in the early days.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.090
Well, laser tag today has moved

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.700
into family entertainment centers,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.490
with actually more people
playing than ever before.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.220
- [Narrator] And there
it is, your weekend,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.079
and some of the people behind it, 1993.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:33.429
♪ All the cats ♪

00:02:33.429 --> 00:02:34.512
- [Kids] Yay!

00:02:36.508 --> 00:02:39.091
(light music)

